The project commits to 3 inter-related outcomes:
1. A project management and digital oversight dashboard to assist in the coordination of diverse projects and stakeholders, and measure progress towards the implementation of the objectives, as outlined in the draft national digital strategy;
2. An Electronic Identification (E-ID), digital application, supported by a National Trust Solution and integrated with the national interoperability framework, to increase the ease of access and efficiency of Government Services for members of society;
3. Digitally transforming the operations of the MSDFS with the potential of expanding and replicating those services at other ministries.
All related procurement of hardware and software is included, along with training in the new systems for relevant government employees as needed.

he project objective is to operationalize integrated agroecosystem management through mainstreaming biodiversity conservation in production landscapes and increasing the resilience of agricultural systems. This will be achieved through the following four interrelated outcomes:
a) Outcome 1: Systemic and institutional capacity for integrated landscape management at the national level;
b) Outcome 2: National capacity to provide financial, technical, and information services for CSA production;
c) Outcome 3: Operationalization of resilient agricultural practices; and
d) Outcome 4: Knowledge management for SLM, CSA, and biodiversity conservation.

The project purpose is to provide technical assistance to Trinidad and Tobago (T&T) in the design of a comprehensive National Trade Strategy (NTS). 
The NTS seeks to bring about a concerted national vision on how to enhance T&T's trade competitiveness, particularly for SMEs, and a coherent strategic framework for trade development. Outcomes also include the building of capacities among national stakeholders to design and update the National Trade Strategy in the future.
The ultimate goal is to mainstream trade in T&T's economic planning, with a view to diversifying the economy, strengthening SMEs competitiveness and trade/investment support institutions, and focusing on priority sectors and markets.
Broadly, the NTS will provide:
A comprehensive strategic framework for export development, economic diversification, and SME competitiveness, including up to 6 sector strategies and up to 4 trade-support-function strategies (the total number of strategies will equal 10 but the number of sector and function strategies will depend on needs);
An informed policy framework to support development and competitiveness of the export sector with a positive human development impact;
More effective, and increased capacity of private and public institutions to support and stimulate exports through the provision of relevant and accessible trade support services;
A framework for more competitive SME capable of connecting to national, regional and global value-chains.

The iCOAST programme aims to drive change in the tourism sector by creating an enabling policy and regulatory environment – supported by financial mechanisms and products (1) that supports and incentivizes governments, private sector and local communities to introduce and benefit from sustainable tourism practices, (2) drives the development of local sustainable supply chains, and (3) makes it convenient for tourists to make sustainable choices.

There are 4 programme components: Policy and Regulations; Finance and Investment; Technical Assistance and Introduction of Alternative Practices, Technologies and Business Models; and Global Coordination and Knowledge Dissemination;

The programme approach addresses the following:
Environmental sustainability – plastic pollution, food waste, energy savings, water and waste management innovation;
Social sustainability – community engagement, capacity building job creation, entrepreneurship;
Economic sustainability – supporting local suppliers, fair trade practices and procurement, sustainable investments;
Enhancing resilience – conservation of cultural and natural heritage, benefit sharing of tourism, better positioning of destination.

